<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The Windows installer for Maxon: one MSI, per-machine, modelled on Go's.

  ⛔ PER-MACHINE, NOT PER-USER, AND THAT IS A DECISION RATHER THAN A DEFAULT. A compiler is a tool a
  machine has, not a tool a user has: build agents, scheduled jobs and other accounts all need to find
  it, and a per-user install puts it on exactly one account's PATH. The cost is a UAC prompt on
  install, which is also what winget's `Scope: machine` expects.

  ⛔ THE INSTALL DIRECTORY PROPERTY IS NAMED `INSTALLDIR`, AND THE NAME IS LOAD-BEARING. winget passes
  `INSTALLDIR="<path>"` through `InstallerSwitches.InstallLocation`, so winget's `install` location
  flag works only if the property is spelled exactly this. Rename it and the flag is silently ignored:
  accepted, reported as success, and the install lands somewhere else.

  ⚠ THE `UpgradeCode` GUID IS FIXED FOREVER. It is the identity every future version upgrades FROM;
  changing it turns an upgrade into a second, parallel installation of Maxon. It is committed here
  rather than generated so that no build can invent one.

  The version comes in as `-d Version=` from `build.sh`, which reads it off the compiler binary.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:ui="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/ui">
  <Package
      Name="Maxon"
      Manufacturer="Maxon Language"
      Version="$(Version)"
      UpgradeCode="C5A7D00A-2A15-4766-A772-4526B96EE3F7"
      Scope="perMachine"
      Compressed="yes">

    <SummaryInformation Description="The Maxon compiler" Manufacturer="Maxon Language" />

    <!-- Refuses to install over a NEWER Maxon rather than silently downgrading one. -->
    <MajorUpgrade DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of Maxon is already installed. Uninstall it first if you mean to go back." />

    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" />

    <!-- ⚠ x64 ONLY. There is no arm64-windows target, so unlike Go there is one architecture to ship
         and no second MSI to keep in step. -->
    <StandardDirectory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
      <Directory Id="INSTALLDIR" Name="Maxon" />
    </StandardDirectory>

    <!-- ⭐ HARVESTED, NOT ENUMERATED. `stdlib/` is hundreds of files and every one of them must ship;
         a hand-written component list is a list that goes stale the first time a module is added, and
         nothing would say so — the install would simply be missing a file the compiler needs. -->
    <ComponentGroup Id="Payload" Directory="INSTALLDIR">
      <Files Include="payload\**" />
    </ComponentGroup>

    <!-- ⛔ HKLM, `Part="last"`, AND NOT PERMANENT. System PATH because the install is per-machine;
         appended rather than prepended so an existing toolchain keeps precedence over a new arrival;
         removed on uninstall, because a PATH entry pointing at a deleted directory is litter every
         later shell pays for. -->
    <Component Id="PathEntry" Directory="INSTALLDIR" Guid="11B495BD-69E1-46FC-912F-5D8F6C90F294">
      <Environment Id="MaxonPath" Name="PATH" Value="[INSTALLDIR]" Part="last" Action="set" System="yes" Permanent="no" />
    </Component>

    <Feature Id="Main" Title="Maxon compiler" Level="1">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="Payload" />
      <ComponentRef Id="PathEntry" />
    </Feature>

    <!-- No Start Menu shortcut: this is a command-line tool and a shortcut to it opens a window that
         prints usage and closes. Add/Remove Programs comes free from the package identity above. -->

    <!--
      ================= Visual Studio Code =================

      ⭐ THE OFFER IS MADE ONLY WHEN VS CODE IS ACTUALLY THERE. A checkbox for an editor the machine
      does not have is an install that fails after the installer said it succeeded, so the whole
      offer — the checkbox and its label — appears only if one of the searches below found a
      `code.cmd` to run.

      ⚠ IT LOOKS FOR `bin\code.cmd`, NOT FOR `Code.exe`. The `.exe` is an Electron shell; the CLI is
      `cli.js` run through it, and `code.cmd` is the launcher that knows how. Handing `Code.exe` an
      install-extension argument opens a window instead of installing anything.

      Three searches, because VS Code has three plausible locations: the two installers' defaults, and
      whatever a system install was redirected to.
    -->
    <Property Id="VSCODE_USER_CMD">
      <DirectorySearch Id="VSCodeUserBin" Path="[LocalAppDataFolder]Programs\Microsoft VS Code\bin" Depth="0">
        <FileSearch Id="VSCodeUserCmd" Name="code.cmd" />
      </DirectorySearch>
    </Property>

    <Property Id="VSCODE_MACHINE_CMD">
      <DirectorySearch Id="VSCodeMachineBin" Path="[ProgramFiles64Folder]Microsoft VS Code\bin" Depth="0">
        <FileSearch Id="VSCodeMachineCmd" Name="code.cmd" />
      </DirectorySearch>
    </Property>

    <!-- A system install put somewhere other than Program Files. The key is the VS Code installer's
         own Inno Setup AppId, which is stable across versions. -->
    <Property Id="VSCODE_REG_CMD">
      <RegistrySearch Id="VSCodeMachineKey"
                      Root="HKLM"
                      Key="SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Uninstall\{EA457B21-F73E-494C-ACAB-524FDE069978}_is1"
                      Name="InstallLocation"
                      Type="directory"
                      Bitness="always64">
        <DirectorySearch Id="VSCodeRegBin" Path="bin" Depth="0">
          <FileSearch Id="VSCodeRegCmd" Name="code.cmd" />
        </DirectorySearch>
      </RegistrySearch>
    </Property>

    <!-- ⛔ THE USER INSTALL WINS. A machine can carry both, and the per-user copy is the one whose
         extensions directory belongs to the person clicking Finish. -->
    <SetProperty Id="VSCODECMD" Action="SetVSCodeCmdFromReg"     Value="[VSCODE_REG_CMD]"     After="AppSearch" Sequence="ui" Condition="VSCODE_REG_CMD" />
    <SetProperty Id="VSCODECMD" Action="SetVSCodeCmdFromMachine" Value="[VSCODE_MACHINE_CMD]" After="SetVSCodeCmdFromReg" Sequence="ui" Condition="VSCODE_MACHINE_CMD" />
    <SetProperty Id="VSCODECMD" Action="SetVSCodeCmdFromUser"    Value="[VSCODE_USER_CMD]"    After="SetVSCodeCmdFromMachine" Sequence="ui" Condition="VSCODE_USER_CMD" />

    <!-- WixUI shows the exit-dialog checkbox only when its TEXT property has a value, so setting the
         text IS the decision to make the offer. -->
    <SetProperty Id="WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOXTEXT"
                 Action="SetVSCodeOfferText"
                 Value="Install the Maxon extension for Visual Studio Code"
                 After="SetVSCodeCmdFromUser" Sequence="ui" Condition="VSCODECMD" />
    <SetProperty Id="WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOX"
                 Action="SetVSCodeOfferDefault"
                 Value="1"
                 After="SetVSCodeOfferText" Sequence="ui" Condition="VSCODECMD" />

    <!--
      ⛔ IT RUNS FROM THE EXIT DIALOG, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES IT LAND IN THE RIGHT PROFILE. A
      per-machine MSI's execute sequence is elevated — under `winget`, under SYSTEM — and VS Code
      installs extensions into the *invoking* account's `~/.vscode/extensions`. An action sequenced
      there would install the extension for whoever elevated the package, which on a build agent is
      nobody. The UI sequence runs unelevated as the person at the keyboard, so the extension lands
      where they will look for it.

      ⇒ It also means a SILENT install never runs this. `msiexec /quiet` and `winget install` have no
      UI sequence at all, so they install the compiler and nothing else — which is the correct
      behaviour for an unattended install and needs no separate guard.

      ⚠ `code.cmd` IS A BATCH FILE, so it goes through `cmd.exe`: `CreateProcess` cannot execute a
      `.cmd` directly. `/s` makes cmd strip exactly the outer pair of quotes, which is what lets a
      quoted path carry spaces.
    -->
    <SetProperty Id="VSCODESHELL" Value="[System64Folder]cmd.exe" After="SetVSCodeOfferDefault" Sequence="ui" Condition="VSCODECMD" />

    <CustomAction Id="InstallVSCodeExtension"
                  Property="VSCODESHELL"
                  ExeCommand="/s /c &quot;&quot;[VSCODECMD]&quot; --install-extension maxon-lang.maxon-lsp-client --force&quot;"
                  Execute="immediate"
                  Impersonate="yes"
                  Return="asyncNoWait" />

    <ui:WixUI Id="WixUI_InstallDir" InstallDirectory="INSTALLDIR" />

    <UI>
      <Publish Dialog="ExitDialog" Control="Finish" Event="DoAction" Value="InstallVSCodeExtension"
               Condition="WIXUI_EXITDIALOGOPTIONALCHECKBOX = 1 AND NOT Installed" />
    </UI>

    <!-- Generated by build.sh from LICENSE-MIT and LICENSE-APACHE, so the dialog cannot show a
         licence the package does not ship. -->
    <WixVariable Id="WixUILicenseRtf" Value="license.rtf" />
  </Package>
</Wix>
